{
  "answer": "Evidence table below. Key finding: since the source corpus at `data/sangraha_hi_verified_30m/raw.jsonl` is Sangraha **Verified** Hindi text (already scraped+cleaned via AI4Bharat's Webcorpus+Setu pipeline), true 'extraction' work for the notebook is largely re-deriving/demonstrating that upstream pipeline on already-extracted JSONL — there is no raw HTML/WARC in scope unless you pull fresh Common Crawl or PDF/OCR data. Recommendation: use Trafilatura (bench-mark leader, actively maintained, Unicode-safe) as the primary HTML/WARC extractor for any supplementary raw-HTML demonstration; use jusText as a second, older heuristic baseline for contrast; treat Boilerpipe as historical/non-installable-by-default reference only; mention Resiliparse/Inscriptis as high-performance alternatives; cite CCNet for the perplexity-filtering methodology Sangraha itself reuses; cite IndicLLMSuite/Sangraha as the actual data-generating pipeline (ACL 2024 Outstanding Paper); treat MinerU-HTML/AICC as a 2025 state-of-the-art model-based alternative (not Hindi-specific, not used by Sangraha); flag SCRIBES as **irrelevant** to Hindi web-text extraction — it is a script-based semi-structured (schema.org/JSON-LD-style) extraction RL framework, not a document/language corpus tool, and no Indic-specific claims exist for it in the source paper.\n\n| Tool/Paper | Verified capability | Hindi/Indic caveat | Suggested notebook role | Verified claims about maintenance/benchmarks | URL |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Trafilatura** (Barbaresi) | Python+CLI, HTML/URL/WARC input → main-content text+metadata extraction, CSV/JSON/MD/TXT/XML output. Actively maintained (v2.1.0 docs current), GPL-3.0, `pip install trafilatura`. | Language-agnostic HTML parsing via lxml/UTF-8; optional `--target-language` filter uses **py3langid** (97 languages incl. Hindi `hi`) but this is opt-in post-filtering, not extraction-quality tuning — no Devanagari-specific extraction logic documented. | Primary extractor if demonstrating raw-HTML→text on a small supplementary Hindi web sample (e.g., re-scrape a few Sangraha source URLs) to show \"how extraction would work.\" | Cited \"most efficient in ScrapingHub benchmark\" and \"best tool in Lejeune & Barbaresi eval\" — these are claims **made in Trafilatura's own README/citation**, not independently reproduced by us; treat as tool-author-asserted, not blind third-party verified. | https://github.com/adbar/trafilatura ; https://trafilatura.readthedocs.io/ |\n| **jusText** (Pomikálek/Belica) | Stopword-density heuristic boilerplate remover, HTML→paragraphs w/ boilerplate flag. Actively maintained fork (miso-belica), latest PyPI release Feb 25 2025, BSD-2-Clause. | Requires a per-language stopword list (`justext.get_stoplist(lang)`); **no built-in Hindi/Devanagari stopword list is confirmed** — must check `justext/stoplists/` for `Hindi.txt` before use; if absent, algorithm silently degrades (falls back to length-only heuristics). | Secondary/contrast extractor to show a classical stopword-heuristic baseline vs. Trafilatura on the same HTML sample — **only if a Hindi stoplist is confirmed present**, otherwise flag as unusable out-of-box for Hindi. | Confirmed actively maintained (2025 release), not a \"dead classic\" as commonly assumed. | https://github.com/miso-belica/jusText |\n| **Boilerpipe** (Kohlschütter et al., 2010) | Java library + shallow-text-feature heuristics (link density, text density, capitalization) for boilerplate removal. Apache 2.0. | No language-specific tokenization awareness in the original algorithm; migrated from Google Code, **last substantive release 2011** — effectively unmaintained/archived, no active Python bindings from the original authors (only third-party ports like `boilerpipepy`). | **Historical/citation-only** — reference in notebook's literature review as the foundational shallow-feature method CCNet/Trafilatura build on; do not install/run for the case study (no maintained Python-native path). | Verified: last release 1.2.0, 2011-06-06; GitHub repo explicitly labeled \"work-in-progress transmit from Google Code.\" | https://github.com/kohlschutter/boilerpipe |\n| **Resiliparse** (ChatNoir/Webis) | C++/Cython (Lexbor-based) HTML parsing + `extract_plain_text`, WARC parsing via FastWARC (WARC/1.0 & 1.1, gzip/LZ4). Actively maintained, current docs v1.0.8. | Extraction is DOM/whitespace-based, script-agnostic — no documented Devanagari-specific caveats found, but also **no explicit multilingual benchmark** in the docs reviewed; benchmarked only on speed (docs/s), not extraction quality by language. | Optional high-performance alternative if the notebook processes WARC files directly (this corpus is JSONL, not WARC, so likely unused unless doing a raw-CC demo). | Verified benchmark figure (1149.56 docs/s vs BS4 48.05 docs/s) comes from Resiliparse's own docs — self-reported, not independently reproduced. | https://github.com/chatnoir-eu/chatnoir-resiliparse ; https://resiliparse.chatnoir.eu/ |\n| **Inscriptis** (weblyzard) | Pure-Python layout-aware HTML→text (preserves tables/whitespace structure), JOSS-reviewed and published (2021), still active (releases through Nov 2025). | No Hindi/Devanagari-specific claims found; layout-awareness (preserving visual line breaks) could help with code-mixed/table-heavy Indic pages but this is inferred, not documented. | Optional mention as a lightweight layout-preserving alternative; not required for this corpus (already-extracted JSONL). | JOSS peer review confirms real maintenance/quality bar, unlike some hobby scrapers. | https://github.com/weblyzard/inscriptis ; https://github.com/openjournals/joss-reviews/issues/3557 |\n| **CCNet** (Wenzek et al., LREC 2020 / arXiv:1911.00359) | Pipeline: language ID (fastText lid.176, 176 languages incl. Hindi `hi`) + perplexity filtering against Wikipedia-trained n-gram LM + dedup, applied to raw Common Crawl. Input = **WET/WARC-derived plaintext**, not raw HTML (relies on CC's own extraction). | Hindi is in fastText's 176-language list (confirmed from fasttext.cc docs), so CCNet's LID step is Hindi-capable; **Sangraha explicitly reuses CCNet's perplexity-filtering code** (not its HTML extraction, since CCNet doesn't do HTML extraction itself — it consumes Common Crawl's WET text dumps). | Cite as the methodological ancestor of Sangraha-Unverified's quality filtering; do not present CCNet as an HTML extractor — it is a **filtering/dedup pipeline over pre-extracted plaintext**. | Paper verified: LREC 2020 (ACL Anthology 2020.lrec-1.494), arXiv v2 posted 2019-11-15. | https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.00359 ; https://aclanthology.org/2020.lrec-1.494/ ; https://github.com/facebookresearch/cc_net |\n| **IndicLLMSuite / Sangraha** (Khan et al., arXiv:2403.06350, ACL 2024 Outstanding Paper) | The actual source of this corpus. Sangraha-Verified-Hindi = web pages scraped via AI4Bharat's own **Webcorpus** framework (not Trafilatura/jusText/Boilerpipe/Resiliparse) + Setu (Spark-based) cleaning/filtering/dedup pipeline, plus human URL-verification and post-cleaning human audit. Total Sangraha = 251B tokens, 22 languages incl. Hindi (`hin`). | Confirmed Hindi is one of 22 listed languages. Toxicity/NSFW wordlists exist per-language (17 languages) — **verify Hindi is among the 17** before assuming toxic-word filtering was applied to this corpus (not directly confirmed in README enumeration). | **Primary literature citation** for the notebook — this is the actual provenance of `raw.jsonl`; the notebook should describe Webcorpus+Setu, not claim Trafilatura/jusText were used, since no evidence supports that. | Verified: ACL 2024 Outstanding Paper Award (aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.843), arXiv 2403.06350 (Mar 2024), GitHub 412 stars. | https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.06350 ; https://github.com/AI4Bharat/IndicLLMSuite ; https://huggingface.co/datasets/ai4bharat/sangraha ; https://github.com/AI4Bharat/webcorpus ; https://github.com/AI4Bharat/setu |\n| **MinerU-HTML / AICC** (arXiv:2511.16397, Nov 2025) | 0.6B-param LM-based sequence-labeling HTML extractor; claims 81.8% ROUGE-N F1 vs Trafilatura 63.6% on MainWebBench (7,887 pages, **English-centric benchmark, script/language mix not confirmed as Indic-representative**); used to build AICC, a 7.3T-token *multilingual* CC corpus (372B tokens after filtering). | No Hindi-specific benchmark figures found in the paper/repo — \"multilingual\" claim is at the corpus level (AICC), not validated per-language; the 81.8%-vs-63.6% figure is **not verifiable as applicable to Devanagari/Hindi pages** specifically. Unrelated to Sangraha. | Cite only as a 2025 SOTA contrast point in the literature review (\"newer model-based extractors exist but lack demonstrated Hindi-specific evaluation\"); do not use for this notebook's extraction step (heavy GPU dependency, no proven Hindi advantage). | Verified paper exists (arXiv 2511.16397, Nov 2025), repo at opendatalab/MinerU-HTML is real and installable. Benchmark numbers are self-reported by the paper's authors, not independently reproduced by us. | https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.16397 ; https://github.com/opendatalab/MinerU-HTML ; https://huggingface.co/datasets/opendatalab/AICC |\n| **SCRIBES** (arXiv:2510.01832, Oct 2025) | RL-trained framework for **web-scale script-based semi-structured data extraction** (e.g., generating extraction scripts for schema.org/structured triples from web pages), evaluated on a CommonCrawl subset (113k pages, 2.79M triples extracted). | **No Hindi/Indic content, benchmark, or claim exists in this paper at all** — it targets structured-data (triple) extraction, not plain-text/prose corpus extraction, and is not language-specific. The user's inclusion of it alongside Trafilatura/CCNet/Sangraha is a **category mismatch**: it solves a different problem (structured data extraction from templated pages, e.g. product/review sites) not full-text web-corpus extraction for LLM pretraining. | **Flag as not applicable** to this notebook; omit from the case study or mention only as \"unrelated adjacent technique\" in the literature survey if the assignment wants breadth. | Paper exists and is real (arXiv 2510.01832), but any claim treating it as a Hindi/Indic corpus-extraction tool is **unsupported/misleading**. | https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01832 |\n\n**Explicit flags on unverified/misleading claims:**\n1. Any assumption that Sangraha-Hindi/raw.jsonl was produced using Trafilatura, jusText, or Boilerpipe is **unverified/false** — the README explicitly names Webcorpus (AI4Bharat's own scraper) + Setu, with no mention of these third-party libraries.\n2. Boilerpipe should not be described as 'currently used' anywhere — last release was 2011, repo is an inert Google Code mirror.\n3. MinerU-HTML/AICC's 81.8%-vs-63.6% ROUGE-N figures are real (arXiv:2511.16397) but were measured on MainWebBench, not on any Hindi/Devanagari-labeled subset — do not port this number to justify Hindi extraction quality claims.\n4. SCRIBES has zero documented relevance to Hindi or prose-text corpus extraction; including it as a comparable tool to Trafilatura/CCNet is a category error and should be corrected or dropped.\n5. Trafilatura's 'best tool' and 'most efficient' benchmark claims trace back to the project's own README/citation (a paper co-authored by the maintainer), not an independent third party — present as author-reported, not neutral ground truth.\n6. CCNet is not an HTML extractor; it operates on Common Crawl's pre-extracted WET plaintext plus fastText LID and Wikipedia-perplexity filtering — don't conflate it with HTML/boilerplate-removal tools in the notebook's tool taxonomy.",
