{
  "answer": "## Comparative matrix — heuristic HTML boilerplate/main-content extractors\n\n| Extractor | Core signal | Required input | Output granularity | Multilingual / Hindi dependence | Failure modes | Resource cost |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **JusText** (Pomikálek, 2011 PhD thesis, Masaryk Univ.; impl. `miso-belica/jusText`) | Blocks HTML on visually-block tags (`<p>`,`<div>`,`<li>`,`<table>`…), then classifies each block by (a) **link density** = fraction of chars inside `<a>` and (b) **stopword density** = fraction of tokens in a per-language stopword list, with context-sensitive re-classification propagating 'good'/'bad' labels to neighboring 'short'/'near-good' blocks | Raw HTML (DOM-adjacent; needs tag boundaries) | Paragraph/block-level text | **Hard dependency on a curated stopword list per language.** Ships Hindi (`hindi` under `jusText/stoplists`) but tokenization is whitespace-split — this is lossy for Devanagari where words carry conjuncts/matras but generally no spaces are lost; ZWJ/ZWNJ chars are invisible joiners inside words so whitespace tokenization does not split on them, but no explicit normalization step exists, so any prior Unicode NFC/NFKC transform that mangles ZWJ/ZWNJ before jusText runs would corrupt stopword matching | Fails on JS-rendered/SPA pages (empty DOM), on pages using generic `<div>` soup with no block tags, and on short-form/dialogue content misclassified as boilerplate; stopword-density approach is inherently biased toward long, sentence-like registers, hurting extraction of tables, lists, code | Pure-Python/regex, single-pass over DOM tree — cheap per doc but slow at Common-Crawl scale relative to Resiliparse |\n| **Boilerpipe** (Kohlschütter, Fankhauser, Nejdl, WSDM 2010, *\"Boilerplate Detection using Shallow Text Features\"*) | Per-block **shallow text features**: text density (words per line char budget), average word length, link density, and **local context features (features of the immediately preceding/following block)** fed into a classifier (decision tree in the paper) — theoretically grounded in quantitative-linguistics text-generation stats, explicitly claims no need for stopword lists or training at token level | Raw HTML → block segmentation off DOM structure, same as JusText | Block-level text | Language-agnostic by design (its stated advantage over JusText) — no stopword list needed, so no direct Hindi tokenization issue; but its density features (words/line, avg word length) were tuned on English web boilerplate and are not re-validated for Devanagari script or code-mixed pages | Density thresholds tuned on a specific (largely English news) corpus; degrades on non-Latin scripts and non-Western page layouts not represented in training data; Java, largely unmaintained since ~2015 | Java lib, block-classifier is cheap (decision tree), but requires a DOM parser (mostly `NekoHTML`/Jsoup) — heavier dependency footprint than JusText for a Python pipeline |\n| **Trafilatura** (Barbaresi, ACL-IJCNLP 2021 System Demos, *\"Trafilatura: A Web Scraping Library and Command-Line Tool for Text Discovery and Extraction\"*, aclanthology.org/2021.acl-demo.15) | Combines XPath/DOM heuristics (tag semantics: `<article>`,`<main>`, common CMS class/id patterns) with **fallback cascade** through other extractors (readability-lxml, justext) and its own density/link-ratio filters; also extracts metadata (title, author, date) and comments separately | Raw HTML, ideally with the original URL for metadata heuristics; works on saved HTML without URL but metadata extraction degrades | Full-document main text + optional metadata/comments, output as TXT/MD/XML/JSON | No stopword dependency for its core heuristics (tag/CSS-class based), so **language-agnostic in the same way as Boilerpipe**, but its own README states it does **not** apply script-specific tokenization — safe for Devanagari as long as no naive space-based token counting occurs internally for link-density; author reports competitive accuracy across languages in ACL demo eval, though eval set is Latin-script-heavy | Depends on markup conventions (CMS boilerplate classes) common on Western commercial sites; less validated on Indian regional-language sites, forums, and government portals with idiosyncratic markup; fallback chain adds complexity/latency | ~4,000 lines of Python, `lxml`-based — fast, single most complete OSS heuristic pipeline; moderate CPU cost per doc, no GPU |\n| **Readability-style DOM extraction** (Mozilla `readability.js`, github.com/mozilla/readability — Arc90 Readability algorithm lineage) | Scores every candidate node (mostly `<p>`) using comma count, class/id name pattern-matching (\"article\", \"content\" vs \"comment\", \"sidebar\"), text length, and **link density** (`_getLinkDensity`, penalizes >0.25 by default), then **propagates the score up the DOM tree** (parent gets more, grandparent less) to find the single container that \"owns\" the article | Raw HTML (JS/DOM-tree operations, uses live `Node`/`Element` tree, not just tag strings) | One \"main content\" HTML fragment (article container) | Class/id name heuristics ('article','content','byline','comment','sidebar' etc.) are **English word lists baked into the source** (`REGEXPS` in Readability.js) — will silently fail to boost/penalize on Hindi-language sites using Devanagari class names, though most Hindi news CMSs still use English/Latin class names, so partial coverage is likely; comma-count heuristic assumes Latin/Devanagari danda `।` is not counted as a sentence-boundary comma equivalent, understating text-like scores for Hindi paragraphs unless configured | Needs a full DOM (typically via `jsdom` in Node, or a Python DOM port); unlikely to work on malformed HTML without a lenient parser; scoring thresholds (`minScore=20`, `minContentLength=140`) are heuristics tuned on English news layouts | JS/DOM-tree traversal is heavier per document than pure regex/text approaches (multiple tree walks, node cloning); no native Python primary implementation — ports (`readability-lxml`, `readabilipy`) trail upstream |\n| **Resiliparse** (Bevendorff, Potthast, Stein — ECIR 2018 *\"Elastic ChatNoir\"* demo paper + OSSYM 2021 *\"FastWARC\"* paper; docs at resiliparse.chatnoir.eu) | Cython/C++-optimized DOM parser + **simple tag- and regex-based rules** (explicitly *not* a trained classifier) for main-content extraction, encoding detection, and content-type sniffing — built for raw Common Crawl WARC/WET-scale throughput rather than max accuracy | Raw HTML/WARC records (built for CC record streams; also standalone HTML) | Main text string (+ optional metadata) | No language-specific stopword or class-name dependency documented; being a lighter/faster ruleset than Trafilatura, empirically shown (OpenWebMath, ICLR 2024) to have similar or better boilerplate/runtime tradeoff than JusText, but **no published multilingual/Hindi-specific accuracy breakdown** was found in the papers — accuracy on Indic scripts is inferred, not measured, in primary sources | Rule simplicity trades some accuracy for speed vs Trafilatura/Boilerpipe on complex layouts; being tag/regex-based (not trained), it inherits the same 'needs real markup' limitation as all DOM-based tools | Fastest of the five by a wide margin (native C++/Cython) — designed explicitly for CC-scale batch processing; best throughput-per-token, lowest per-doc memory |\n\n## Feasibility given the actual Sangraha cache\n\nAll five families are **DOM/tag-driven at their core signal**: JusText and Boilerpipe segment on block-level tags and measure link/stopword/text density inside those blocks; Trafilatura's heuristics key on tag semantics (`<article>`, CMS class names) and its own fallback chain of DOM-based extractors; Readability-style extraction literally requires a live DOM tree and node-level score propagation; Resiliparse parses raw HTML/WARC and applies tag-aware regex rules. **None of the five can run meaningfully on a flattened `text` column** — the `stepwise_cleaning.py` cache (`doc_id`, `type`, `text`, `estimated_tokens`; confirmed by reading the notebook's `download_setup`/`download_sample` cells) has already discarded HTML, URLs, and WARC records at the AI4Bharat/Sangraha ingestion stage, so there is no DOM, no `<a>` tag to compute link density, no block boundary to segment on, and no CSS class/id to pattern-match. Running any of these tools against the cache would require either (a) re-crawling/matching the original URLs back to raw HTML (Sangraha does not publish a per-doc URL/WARC pointer in the `verified` config's `hin` split schema, so this is not a lightweight retrofit) or (b) faking pseudo-blocks by splitting the flattened text on blank lines/newlines and reusing only the *density* half of the algorithms (stopword-density, link-density-would-be-zero, text-density) — which is a materially different, weaker heuristic than what any of the five papers describe and validate.\n\n## Recommendation for the 30M-token Hindi notebook\n\n- **Do not add JusText/Boilerpipe/Trafilatura/Readability/Resiliparse as extraction stages.** They are solutions to a problem (raw-HTML boilerplate) this cache no longer has; Sangraha's own pipeline already performed this extraction upstream, and re-deriving it from flattened text is infeasible without the original HTML/WARC.\n- If residual boilerplate patterns are still visible in `text` (nav-menu fragments, repeated footers, share-widget text), the only legitimately transferable *ideas* from this literature are the **density heuristics stripped of their DOM dependency**: (1) stopword-density scoring per newline-delimited pseudo-block using a Hindi stopword list (JusText's technique, reusable at the line level) and (2) short-line / high-symbol-ratio heuristics analogous to Boilerpipe's shallow text features. These should be scoped as a **quality-filtering** cell (already stage 4 of the notebook's eight-stage checklist), not relabeled as \"extraction\" — extraction is already complete and out of scope per the assignment's own constraints.\n- Any Hindi stopword list used for this must be verified separately (this is the ToolsLiteratureAudit / HindiExtractionDesign lane's concern) for ZWJ/ZWNJ-safe tokenization, since JusText's default whitespace tokenizer does not normalize joiners and code-mixed (Hindi+English) lines will under-count Hindi stopwords if English tokens dominate a line.\n- Net effect on the notebook: **no new extraction cell**; at most a short markdown note in the existing pipeline documenting why HTML-heuristic extractors were evaluated and found inapplicable, plus optional reuse of stopword-density as one signal inside the existing quality-filtering stage.\n",
