{
  "summary": "Design for a rigorous, Hindi-safe, extraction-only evaluation of the 50,952-doc / ~30M-token Sangraha-verified/hin sample at data/sangraha_hi_verified_30m/raw.jsonl. Central verified fact that shapes the whole design: the JSONL has only three fields — doc_id, type ({web, pdf} in this sample), text. No source URL, raw HTML, PDF binary, or JSON-LD is stored (confirmed by direct read of the file and by AI4Bharat's README at huggingface.co/datasets/ai4bharat/sangraha). AI4Bharat's own pipeline already ran DOM extraction (web) or OCR (pdf) upstream and discarded provenance before release. Consequently this assignment cannot perform primary DOM/HTML extraction on the corpus itself — it must be a residual-artifact audit of an already-extracted corpus, cross-checked against a small live-refetched HTML side-channel. Six staged checks below, each with deterministic baselines, notebook-observable metrics, explicit pass/fail examples, sample-size guidance, and what must not be claimed. A minimal implementation for the existing raw JSONL closes the design.",
  "architecture": "STAGE 1 Source classification: groupby `type` (deterministic, no baseline needed). PASS = every record has type in the closed observed set {web,pdf}; FAIL = any other/empty type left unexplained. Caveat: type is Sangraha's own ingestion-channel label, not a verified structural classification — do not claim it measures true source diversity.

STAGE 2 DOM/HTML extraction quality: cannot run on this corpus's own HTML (none stored). Draw a stratified sample (n≈150–300 type=web docs) and attempt live re-fetch via a distinctive quoted text substring; expect non-trivial non-recovery (link rot) — report that rate as a metric, not a failure to hide. For recovered pages, run two deterministic extractors (trafilatura, readability-lxml) on the fetched HTML and diff against stored `text` with token-level precision/recall. PASS example: stored text token-recall ≥0.9 vs. trafilatura output on the same live page, no residual markup, no unresolved boilerplate marker hits. FAIL example: stored text contains a bilingual boilerplate-marker hit above the sample noise floor. Critical caveat: this repo's own pipeline-discarded.py extract_stage hard-codes only Latin-script boilerplate markers ("accept cookies", "subscribe", "advertisement") — that lexicon will miss Hindi-language chrome (कुकीज़ स्वीकार करें, विज्ञापन, सदस्यता लें, होम, मुख्य पृष्ठ) and will falsely flag short Hindi function words co-occurring with English loanwords. A pass/fail marker list must be bilingual and reviewed by a Hindi-literate rater before being trusted.

STAGE 3 JSON/embedded-asset handling: same no-local-artifact limitation as Stage 2. On the same re-fetched HTML sub-sample, check whether JSON-LD/infobox key:value content leaked into stored text as raw syntax (`{"@type`, `application/ld+json`, stray `{...}` braces) versus being cleanly rendered as prose, and whether image alt-text or caption JSON was duplicated into the body. Metric: count of docs with raw-syntax leakage per 1k docs; contrast web vs none-expected-for-pdf. PASS = zero raw JSON/script syntax fragments in text. FAIL = any `{"@context"` or `function(` style fragment present verbatim. Do not claim this measures embedded-asset richness of the *source* page — only whether extraction leaked syntax into the delivered corpus.

STAGE 4 Layout/list/table preservation: fully checkable on stored text alone. Heuristic detectors: (a) numbered/bulleted list patterns collapsed onto one line vs. preserved as line breaks (regex for `\d+\.|•|१\.|२\.` immediately followed by more list markers without a newline); (b) table-like `|`-delimited or tab-delimited runs; (c) run-on paragraphs where a PDF's OCR line-wrap produced spurious mid-sentence line breaks (very short lines <15 chars repeated ≥3x in a row, common in doc-type=pdf as seen in this sample). Metric: % of docs with detected list/table flattening; distribution of line-length variance for pdf vs web (pdf should show a bimodal short/long pattern if OCR line-wrapping bled through). PASS = list markers retain a line boundary per item. FAIL = list items concatenated into a single un-delimited run, destroying enumerability. Caveat: Devanagari matras and conjuncts must not be miscounted as separate 'short lines' by a naive character splitter — use grapheme-cluster-aware line/word counting (Python's default `len()` on NFC-normalized text undercounts nothing extra here since Python str is codepoint-based, but visually a single grapheme cluster with combining marks must not be treated as 'noise'; verify with `unicodedata.combining()` before flagging a line as anomalously fragmented).

STAGE 5 Boilerplate detection: distinct from Stage 2's HTML-diff check because it must also run on `type=pdf` docs where there is no HTML at all — OCR headers/footers, page numbers, running heads repeated verbatim across a document's lines are the pdf-specific boilerplate class. Metric: for each doc, count of lines repeated verbatim ≥2x at line-start position — a strong page-header/footer signal in OCR'd PDFs. PASS = repeated-line rate below a corpus-wide 95th-percentile threshold. FAIL = a document where >15% of lines are exact repeats of a short (<40 char) string — almost certainly page furniture. Caveat: do not use a fixed English stopword/short-line length filter as the sole signal — Hindi headers are often short by nature (सम्पादकीय, विषय-सूची); combine repetition-across-document with shortness, not shortness alone, to avoid English-centric overfiltering that would strip legitimate short Hindi headings/verse lines (the corpus contains poetry — see doc example with दोहा/verse structure — where short repeated refrains are content, not boilerplate; a human audit pass must distinguish these).

STAGE 6 Extraction confidence/routing: a synthesized per-document score combining Stage 2 boilerplate-marker-hit-rate (web only, or NaN/pdf-flag when unavailable), Stage 4 list/table-flattening flag, Stage 5 repeated-line rate, and a cheap OCR-garble proxy (ratio of isolated Devanagari matra/vowel-sign codepoints not attached to a preceding consonant — a robust, script-aware OCR-noise signal, computed via a regex requiring every dependent vowel sign U+093E–U+094C and virama U+094D to be immediately preceded by a consonant U+0915–U+0939/U+0958–U+095F). Route: score above threshold → 'needs re-extraction/exclude', below → 'clean enough for downstream cleaning stages'. PASS example: a doc with 0 boilerplate hits, 0 flattening, <5% repeated lines, <1% orphaned matras → auto-pass. FAIL example: a pdf doc with >10% orphaned dependent vowel signs → flag for exclusion or manual OCR review, not automatic deletion, since orphaned-matra rate alone is a proxy, not ground truth.

SAMPLE-SIZE STRATEGY: population 50,952 docs, ~86% web / ~14% pdf by rough inspection of the first 200 lines (verify exact split with a full groupby before finalizing — this repo-visible ratio is from a 200-line preview, not the full file). For a 95% CI with ±5pp margin on a binary pass/fail proportion, n≈380 total; stratify proportionally by type (≈330 web, ≈50 pdf) and, within web, by rough length decile so short stub docs and long articles are both represented. For the Stage 2/3 live-refetch sub-sample, budget for ~30–50% non-recovery (link rot on multi-year-old news URLs) — draw 2x the target recoverable count up front.

WHAT SHOULD NOT BE CLAIMED: (1) that this evaluates the *original* web/PDF source quality — it evaluates Sangraha's already-applied extraction, since no original artifact is retained; (2) that a Latin-script boilerplate/stopword lexicon generalizes to Hindi without a bilingual-marker rebuild and human review; (3) that `type` field reliably predicts extraction-artifact class beyond the two coarse pipelines it names (it says nothing about which CMS/OCR engine, output DPI, or scan quality was used); (4) that orphaned-matra ratio or repeated-line ratio are ground-truth OCR-quality labels — they are cheap proxies requiring the manual audit below before being used to exclude documents; (5) that any metric here measures deduplication, PII, decontamination, or downstream quality filtering — those are explicitly out of scope for this extraction-only design.

MANUAL AUDIT METHODOLOGY: draw the Stage-6 flagged subset plus an equal-size random control from the same strata. Two Hindi-literate raters (or one rater + one gold-check pass) independently label each sampled doc on: extraction-artifact present (y/n + type: nav/boilerplate/JSON-leak/list-flatten/OCR-garble/none), and whether flagged content is genuine noise vs. legitimate short/repeated Hindi text (verse refrain, formulaic legal/government boilerplate that IS the content, e.g. circular headers in the RBI-circular-style doc in this sample). Compute inter-rater agreement (Cohen's κ) before trusting any threshold; a κ<0.6 on the marker lexicon means the automated Stage 2/5 detectors need revision, not the corpus. Thresholds (95th percentile repeated-line rate, 1% orphaned-matra rate, etc.) are calibrated FROM this human-labeled sample, not assumed a priori — report the calibration curve, not just a chosen cutoff.

RECOMMENDED MINIMAL IMPLEMENTATION for the existing raw.jsonl (no re-download, no HTML fetch required for the core pass): a single notebook pass computing, per document, purely from stored `text` — (a) type-conditioned length/line-length distributions (Stage 1+4 proxy), (b) bilingual boilerplate-marker hit count (Stage 2/5 proxy, marker list built from a 100-doc manual skim first), (c) repeated-line ratio (Stage 5), (d) orphaned-dependent-vowel-sign ratio (Stage 6 OCR proxy), (e) raw-JSON/script-syntax-fragment count (Stage 3 proxy) — then a stratified n≈380 live-refetch sub-study (Stage 2/3 ground truth) only for the docs whose automated proxies disagree most sharply from the 'clean' baseline, to keep network calls minimal while still producing a defensible precision/recall number against real HTML. Report all six stage metrics as before/after-nothing counts (this is extraction-diagnosis, not extraction-cleaning) plus the calibration table from the manual audit.",
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "data/sangraha_hi_verified_30m/raw.jsonl",
      "description": "Existing corpus: 50,952 Sangraha verified/hin docs, fields doc_id/type/text only, type∈{web,pdf} observed; no source URL/HTML/PDF/JSON-LD retained — this is the sole local ground truth for extraction-only analysis."
    },
    {
      "path": "pipeline-discarded.py",
      "description": "Prior draft pipeline (lines ~310-353) already implements a naive extract_stage with regex tag-stripping and a Latin-script-only boilerplate lexicon — cited here as the concrete example of the English-centric overfiltering risk the new design must avoid; do not copy its marker list unmodified."
    },
    {
      "path": "AGENTS.md",
      "description": "Confirms assignment intent: this is a hands-on small case study (10–100M tokens), not a production pipeline; explicitly warns against blind ZWJ/ZWNJ stripping, dropping Hinglish, trusting the `hin` label, and English-centric filtering rules — all directly inform the caveats above."
    }
  ]
}

