{
  "answer": "Both AICC/MinerU-HTML (arXiv:2511.16397) and SCRIBES (arXiv:2510.01832) are real, verifiable papers. AICC/MinerU-HTML: a 0.6B-param sequence-labeling LM (fine-tuned Qwen3) for HTML main-content extraction, achieving self-reported 81.8% ROUGE-N F1 vs Trafilatura 63.6% on their own WebMainBench (7,887 pages); code, model weights, and the 7.3T-token AICC corpus are publicly released (GitHub: opendatalab/MinerU-HTML, HF: opendatalab/AICC, Apache-2.0). Its own multilingual metadata scheme only labels pages en/non_en (via GPT-5) — no Hindi-specific evaluation exists in the paper. Compute: at Common-Crawl scale (~300B docs) it uses a clustering trick so LM inference runs on roughly 1 representative page per subdomain cluster, remaining pages handled CPU-only via rule propagation — no GPU-hour/cost figure is given. SCRIBES (Stanford/Meta, ICLR 2026-accepted) is a GRPO reinforcement-learning framework that generates reusable per-site DOM-extraction scripts for semi-structured content (tables/infoboxes/lists), not full-document text extraction; it explicitly restricts its CommonCrawl wild-data pipeline to English-language websites ('we apply language filters to select English content websites'), trained on only 192 annotated pages / 34 site groups plus synthetic CC data. No Hindi evaluation exists, and no public code/model release was found despite targeted search. Both methods require raw HTML/DOM as input and, for SCRIBES, same-site page groupings — neither is runnable against your current Sangraha verified/hin cache, which contains only text, doc_id, and type (no HTML/WARC/URL). This is a hard data-availability wall, not a benchmark-transfer nuance. Compared to heuristic extraction (e.g., Trafilatura/jusText, which your corpus was presumably built with, or similar), the model-based approaches claim quality gains but those gains are self-reported on English-centric benchmarks (WebMainBench/CleanEval-derived) and must not be extrapolated to Hindi or code-mixed text per your constraints — Devanagari script density, ZWJ/ZWNJ handling, and code-mixing were not evaluated by either paper's authors. Recommendation: document both as a future-work comparison paragraph in stepwise_cleaning.py notes, explicitly flagging the unverified-for-Hindi status and the raw-HTML prerequisite; do NOT implement as a runnable notebook experiment against the current cache, since neither method has an executable path against text-only Sangraha data without re-acquiring source HTML.",
